  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nIsentropic upglide associated with an intensifying \nsouthwesterly low-level jet overrunning a quasi-stationary \nsurface boundary over central Illinois continues to facilitate \nblossoming convection from eastern Iowa eastward into portions \nof northern Illinois early this morning. While storm coverage \nsouth of I-80 remains scattered as of this writing, we continue \nto monitor expanding showers and thunderstorms over eastern \nIowa. This activity is expected to spread eastward into northern\nIllinois through the predawn hours as the low-level jet veers. \n\nThe environment in which these storms are developing is \nanomalously moist (precipitable water value near ~2\" per the 00z\nDVN RAOB) with very high freezing levels (just under 15,000 ft)\nsupportive of deep warm cloud depths. Accordingly, very heavy \nrain rates (1+\" per hour) will likely accompany these showers \nand thunderstorms this morning, potentially leading to a few \nbands of 2\"+ amounts (locally higher), particularly where \nmultiple storms train over the same area in northern IL. While \nrainfall of this magnitude could result in localized flash \nflooding, recent dry weather (30-day precipitation deficits of \n10-20% of normal) and low soil moisture may limit this threat to\nsome degree. Nevertheless, we will continue to monitor radar \ntrends for potential hydro issues this morning, particularly in\nthe more urban areas around Rockford and Chicago. Otherwise, \nthe shower and thunderstorm threat is expected to shift eastward\nout of the area by late morning.\n\nAside from a low (~20-30%) chance of a few afternoon and \nevening storms near the surface frontal boundary across central \nIllinois and Indiana, a period of rain-free weather is expected \nthis afternoon through at least the first half of Friday. We \nshould see another fairly large variation in temperatures across\nthe area, particularly this afternoon. Low clouds are likely to\npersist across northern areas (especially along and north of \nI-80), holding temperatures steady in the lower to mid-70s \nthrough the afternoon. Conversely, areas farther south into \ncentral portions of Illinois and Indiana will experience another\nvery warm and humid afternoon, with temperatures and heat \nindices topping out in the mid-to-upper 80s (low 90s).\n\nAttention then turns to another wet period across the region \nthis weekend into early next week. Ensemble guidance remains in \nagreement that the upper-level wave over the western CONUS will \neject a couple of mid/upper-level shortwaves northeastward into \nthe Midwest Friday into the weekend. Increasing upper-level jet \ndiffluence and low-level convergence along the nose of an \nintensifying low-level jet should lead to widespread showers and\nstorms in the Upper Mississippi River Valley Friday into \nSaturday morning. As the parent Pacific wave drifts eastward and\na surface high-pressure system moves into southern Canada this \nweekend, a low-level frontal zone will shift southward across \nthe Lower Great Lakes and serve as a focus for several rounds of\nshowers and storms Saturday afternoon through the middle of \nnext week. We will maintain broad-brushed PoPs from Saturday \nafternoon onward, along with a temperature gradient (coolest \nnorth, warmest south) similar to current conditions.\n\nKJB",
